 "result": "**Mechanism:** Werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects backed by a single `ContextVar`. All of it is in `src/tessel/globals.py`.\n\n- `_app_var` (`globals.py:40`) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` named `\"tessel.app_ctx\"`. It holds the current `AppContext`. The context sets it when pushed and resets it when popped. I didn't open `src/tessel/ctx.py`, so that part is inferred.\n- `active_app` (line 44) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`. It resolves to `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` instance from `src/tessel/app.py`.\n- `g` (line 47) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`. It resolves to `AppContext.g`, an `_AppCtxGlobals` from `src/tessel/ctx.py`.\n- `request` (line 57) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`. It resolves to `AppContext.request`, a `Request` from `src/tessel/wrappers.py`.\n\nEach proxy looks up the `ContextVar` on every access and then reads the named attribute from the current `AppContext`. Each thread or async task therefore sees its own context.\n\nIf no context is active, the proxy raises a `RuntimeError` with the `unbound_message`. `active_app` and `g` use `_no_app_msg`, and `request` uses `_no_req_msg`.\n\n`app_ctx` and `session` use the same mechanism. `request_ctx` is a deprecated alias for `app_ctx`, because the request context has merged into the app context.",
